EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - DAY

heat coming off the asphalt. a line of cars not moving. a dog on a leash sniffs a tire while a man in a vest watches it like it might say something.

Kit, 30s, sits behind the wheel of a dusty hatchback, window down, passport already in hand. Beside her, her brother Leo, 20s, picks at the seam of the seat.

KIT
ok so if he asks, we're visiting our aunt

LEO
we don't have an aunt

KIT
we do now

Officer Daria Okoye, 40s, walks up to the window, clipboard tucked under one arm.

OKOYE
morning. where you headed today

KIT
(too fast)
to see our aunt. she's, um. she's not well.

OKOYE
she have a name

KIT
yeah. it's. Marguerite.

LEO
(at the same moment)
Margaret.

They look at each other. Okoye does not move.

OKOYE
which is it

KIT
both. it's a nickname thing, she goes by both, it's a whole thing in the family

Behind them, a kid in the next car presses his face to the glass. His mom pulls him back. A second officer, Brandt, 50s, drifts toward the hatchback from the other side, hand resting near his belt.

BRANDT
trunk, please

LEO
there's nothing in it

BRANDT
then it'll be quick

Kit and Leo both freeze. The dog lifts its head. Brandt and Okoye exchange a look.

KIT
(very quiet, to Leo)
if you run i swear to god

LEO
(equally quiet)
i'm not running. you're the one who wanted to come this way

Leo pops the trunk. Brandt lifts the lid. Inside: a cooler, a sleeping bag, a framed photograph of an older woman.

BRANDT
(reading the back)
Marguerite Margaret Vance. 1951.

KIT
(exhaling)
told you

Okoye lowers the clipboard. For a second nobody speaks. Then Okoye almost smiles.

OKOYE
go on. drive safe. and say hi to your aunt for me

Kit and Leo both laugh, a little too loud, as the barrier lifts and the cars behind them start to honk.
